{"uid":"1a2a6ced184ef81","name":"test_review_md_and_verdict_gate_md_both_describe_the_disposable_clone_protocol","fullName":"tests.test_review_findings_prompt_shape#test_review_md_and_verdict_gate_md_both_describe_the_disposable_clone_protocol","historyId":"581db28c46039035eaa07807c4572641","time":{"start":1791889995406,"stop":1791889995408,"duration":2},"description":"#208 widened what this node pins, and pins MORE of the section, not\n    less. It used to require the two prompts to spell the protocol out in\n    `ralphd`'s own terms -- the literal `ralphd.__file__`, and \"resolves\n    inside the temp clone's own tree, not the installed engine's copy and not\n    the original workspace\" -- which made the engine's own package name part\n    of the contract every product under review had to satisfy. A product with\n    no `pyproject.toml` cannot, so a hat honouring that prompt measured\n    nothing and rejected `curable: false` on every approach.\n\n    The two halves of the section are now pinned separately, which is what\n    those two literals were standing in for: the INVARIANT (a throwaway\n    clone, plus a *proven* identity claim naming both wrong answers it rules\n    out), and the Python RECIPE as one worked example beside an\n    any-other-ecosystem clause. `git clone --no-hardlinks` and `pip install\n    -e .` survive verbatim, so nothing this node used to catch escapes it;\n    the ecosystem-neutral half is new coverage. The rest of #208's prompt\n    contract -- the operator-authorized protocol, and the `curable: false`\n    prohibition -- is pinned in `tests/test_review_clone_protocol.py`\n    alongside the engine and CLI halves it belongs with.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>#208 widened what this node pins, and pins MORE of the section, not<br />\nless. It used to require the two prompts to spell the protocol out in<br />\n<code>ralphd</code>'s own terms -- the literal <code>ralphd.__file__</code>, and &quot;resolves<br />\ninside the temp clone's own tree, not the installed engine's copy and not<br />\nthe original workspace&quot; -- which made the engine's own package name part<br />\nof the contract every product under review had to satisfy. A product with<br />\nno <code>pyproject.toml</code> cannot, so a hat honouring that prompt measured<br />\nnothing and rejected <code>curable: false</code> on every approach.</p>\n<pre><code>The two halves of the section are now pinned separately, which is what\nthose two literals were standing in for: the INVARIANT (a throwaway\nclone, plus a *proven* identity claim naming both wrong answers it rules\nout), and the Python RECIPE as one worked example beside an\nany-other-ecosystem clause. `git clone --no-hardlinks` and `pip install\n-e .` survive verbatim, so nothing this node used to catch escapes it;\nthe ecosystem-neutral half is new coverage. The rest of #208's prompt\ncontract -- the operator-authorized protocol, and the `curable: false`\nprohibition -- is pinned in `tests/test_review_clone_protocol.py`\nalongside the engine and CLI halves it belongs with.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791889683010,"stop":1791889683010,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791889683011,"stop":1791889683011,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"monkeypatch","time":{"start":1791889995369,"stop":1791889995369,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"at